class.description:
[PerformanceRecorder] records one row per frame (or every few frames, see [member PerformanceRecordingProfile.sample_interval_frames]) with engine timings, [Performance] monitors, process memory, metrics pushed by the game, and values of native metric sources. The rows go into a ring buffer that is allocated when recording starts, and are appended to a CSV file every [member PerformanceRecordingProfile.flush_interval_frames] rows. It works in release builds, without the editor or debugger, on every platform including Web.
Unlike [Performance], whose time monitors are the maximum of the last second, the recorder sees the exact time of every frame. The engine calls it once per frame, after rendering; when not recording, this costs a single check.
Recording can start in three ways, all with the same result: by calling [method start], with the project setting [member ProjectSettings.debug/performance_recording/enabled], or with the command line option [code]--record-performance <file>[/code] (together with [code]--record-performance-profile <file>[/code], [code]--fixed-fps[/code] and [code]--quit-after[/code] for benchmark runs).
[codeblocks]
[gdscript]
var enemies_id = PerformanceRecorder.register_metric(&"enemies_alive")
var spawned_id = PerformanceRecorder.register_metric(&"enemies_spawned", PerformanceRecorder.METRIC_COUNTER)

func _ready():
	PerformanceRecorder.start(load("res://perf/default.tres"))

func _process(_delta):
	PerformanceRecorder.set_metric(enemies_id, enemies.size())

func spawn_enemy():
	PerformanceRecorder.add_metric(spawned_id, 1)
[/gdscript]
[csharp]
private int _enemiesId = PerformanceRecorder.RegisterMetric("enemies_alive");
private int _spawnedId = PerformanceRecorder.RegisterMetric("enemies_spawned", PerformanceRecorder.MetricKind.Counter);

public override void _Ready()
{
	PerformanceRecorder.Start(GD.Load<PerformanceRecordingProfile>("res://perf/default.tres"));
}

public override void _Process(double delta)
{
	PerformanceRecorder.SetMetric(_enemiesId, _enemies.Count);
}

private void SpawnEnemy()
{
	PerformanceRecorder.AddMetric(_spawnedId, 1);
}
[/csharp]
[/codeblocks]
Missing values are written as empty CSV cells and returned as [constant @GDScript.NAN] by [method get_column]: for example a gauge that was never set, or the process memory on a platform that cannot report it.
[b].NET runtime metrics:[/b] in .NET builds of Godot, the C# module registers the metric source [code]"dotnet"[/code] once the .NET runtime is initialized. Add [code]"dotnet"[/code] to [member PerformanceRecordingProfile.metric_sources] to record the columns [code]dotnet/gc_allocated_bytes[/code], [code]dotnet/gc_total_memory_bytes[/code], [code]dotnet/gc_heap_size_bytes[/code], [code]dotnet/gc_fragmented_bytes[/code], [code]dotnet/gc_committed_bytes[/code], [code]dotnet/gc_pause_time_percent[/code], [code]dotnet/gc_pause_total_ms[/code], [code]dotnet/gc_collections_gen0[/code], [code]dotnet/gc_collections_gen1[/code], [code]dotnet/gc_collections_gen2[/code], [code]dotnet/threadpool_threads[/code], [code]dotnet/threadpool_pending[/code], [code]dotnet/threadpool_completed[/code], [code]dotnet/lock_contentions[/code] and [code]dotnet/jit_compiled_methods[/code], plus the per-row deltas [code]dotnet/gc_allocated_bytes_delta[/code], [code]dotnet/gc_collections_delta[/code] and [code]dotnet/gc_pause_delta_ms[/code] (empty in the first row of a recording). Totals count since the start of the process; heap size, fragmented and committed bytes and the pause time percentage describe the last garbage collection. All values are read with a single call into .NET per row, without allocating. The same values (without the deltas) are also available as [Performance] custom monitors [code]dotnet/<name>[/code], for example [code]Performance.get_custom_monitor(&"dotnet/gc_allocated_bytes")[/code]; they are read at most once per frame. The source does not exist on Web (no .NET support) or in builds without .NET; [method has_metric_source] tells whether it is available. On iOS (NativeAOT), [code]jit_compiled_methods[/code] is [code]0[/code]; on Android (Mono runtime), the last-collection values and the pause time can be [code]0[/code].
[b]Note:[/b] On Android, iOS and Web the application can be closed without a clean shutdown. Rows that are not written yet are lost, so call [method flush] when the application is paused (see [constant Node.NOTIFICATION_APPLICATION_PAUSED] and [constant Node.NOTIFICATION_APPLICATION_FOCUS_OUT]).
[b]Note:[/b] On Web, the CSV file lives in IndexedDB ([code]user://[/code]). Every write opens, appends and closes the file, so it is persisted. Read it with [method FileAccess.get_file_as_bytes] and offer it with [method JavaScriptBridge.download_buffer].
[b]Note:[/b] [method start], [method stop], [method flush] and the methods that read recorded data must be called on the main thread. [method register_metric], [method set_metric] and [method add_metric] can be called from any thread.
